-If anything goes wrong, don't panic — escalate to the on-call DBA.
+Verify the fence before continuing:
+
+```bash
+psql "$PRIMARY_URL" -c 'select 1'    # expect: connection refused or timeout
+```
+
+A successful `select 1` here means the primary is still writable. Do not continue.
+
+## Step 4: promote the replica (IRREVERSIBLE)
+
+Confirm all three before running the command:
+
+1. Step 1 showed acceptable lag, and you recorded the LSN.
+2. Step 3's verification failed to connect to the old primary.
+3. You are promoting `db-replica-1`, not `db-replica-2`.
+
+```bash
+ssh db-replica-1.prod.internal 'sudo -u postgres /usr/lib/postgresql/16/bin/pg_ctl promote -D /var/lib/postgresql/16/main'
+```
+
+Record the time. Verify:
+
+```bash
+psql "$REPLICA_URL" -c 'select pg_is_in_recovery()'   # expect: f
+psql "$REPLICA_URL" -c 'select timeline_id from pg_control_checkpoint()'
+```
+
+`pg_is_in_recovery()` returning `f` means promotion completed. The timeline increments by 1; note the new value.
+
+Promotion still in recovery after 60 seconds, check the log:
+
+```bash
+ssh db-replica-1.prod.internal 'sudo tail -50 /var/log/postgresql/postgresql-16-main.log'
+```
+
+A promotion that does not complete is a paging condition. Page the database on-call. Do not attempt to promote `db-replica-2` as well; two promoted hosts is the worst outcome of this procedure.
+
+## Step 5: repoint the connection pooler
+
+Applications reach Postgres through pgbouncer. Update both pgbouncer hosts.
+
+```bash
+for host in pgb-1 pgb-2; do
+  ssh "$host.prod.internal" \
+    "sudo sed -i 's/db-primary.prod.internal/db-replica-1.prod.internal/' /etc/pgbouncer/pgbouncer.ini"
+  ssh "$host.prod.internal" 'sudo pgbouncer -R -d /etc/pgbouncer/pgbouncer.ini || sudo systemctl reload pgbouncer'
+done
+```
+
+`systemctl reload` sends SIGHUP, which applies the new host to new server connections and leaves client connections open.
+
+Confirm each pgbouncer now points at the promoted host:
+
+```bash
+for host in pgb-1 pgb-2; do
+  echo "$host"
+  psql "postgresql://pgbouncer@$host.prod.internal:6432/pgbouncer" -c 'show databases' | grep replica-1
+done
+```
+
+Existing pooled server connections to the fenced primary are dead. Drop them:
+
+```bash
+for host in pgb-1 pgb-2; do
+  psql "postgresql://pgbouncer@$host.prod.internal:6432/pgbouncer" -c 'reconnect'
+done
+```
+
+## Step 6: verify writes
+
+```bash
+psql "postgresql://app@pgb-1.prod.internal:6432/appdb" -c \
+  "create table if not exists failover_probe (id serial primary key, at timestamptz default now())"
+psql "postgresql://app@pgb-1.prod.internal:6432/appdb" -c \
+  "insert into failover_probe default values returning id, at"
+```
+
+The insert returning a row means writes are live. Repeat through `pgb-2`.
+
+Check the application path as well:
+
+```bash
+curl -fsS https://api.prod.example.com/healthz/db
+```
+
+Drop the probe table:
+
+```bash
+psql "postgresql://app@pgb-1.prod.internal:6432/appdb" -c 'drop table failover_probe'
+```
+
+Writes still failing after both pgbouncers reload:
+
+- `server login failed` means the promoted host lacks the app role's `pg_hba.conf` entry. Check `/etc/postgresql/16/main/pg_hba.conf` on `db-replica-1`.
+- `cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction` means step 4 did not complete, or pgbouncer is still routed at a replica. Re-check `pg_is_in_recovery()`.
+
+## Step 7: repoint the second replica
+
+`db-replica-2` is following a host that no longer accepts connections. Point it at the new primary:
+
+```bash
+ssh db-replica-2.prod.internal \
+  "sudo -u postgres psql -c \"alter system set primary_conninfo = 'host=db-replica-1.prod.internal port=5432 user=replicator'\""
+ssh db-replica-2.prod.internal 'sudo -u postgres psql -c "select pg_reload_conf()"'
+```
+
+Confirm it is streaming, from the new primary:
+
+```bash
+psql "$REPLICA_URL" -x -c 'select client_addr, state, sent_lsn, replay_lsn from pg_stat_replication'
+```
+
+`state` of `streaming` means it reattached. A `db-replica-2` that diverged from the new timeline needs a rebuild:
+
+```bash
+ssh db-replica-2.prod.internal 'sudo systemctl stop postgresql@16-main'
+ssh db-replica-2.prod.internal 'sudo -u postgres pg_rewind \
+  --target-pgdata=/var/lib/postgresql/16/main \
+  --source-server="host=db-replica-1.prod.internal user=postgres"'
+ssh db-replica-2.prod.internal 'sudo systemctl start postgresql@16-main'
+```
+
+The cluster runs with one replica until this completes. A second failover has no target in that window.
+
+## After
+
+- Post the promotion timestamp and the LSN from step 1 in the incident channel.
+- Update the DNS record `db-primary.prod.internal` to the promoted host's address, then revert step 5 to the DNS name at the next maintenance window.
+- Leave the old primary fenced. Do not restart it. It holds a diverged timeline, and starting it invites a split brain.
+- File the rebuild of the old primary as a replica as a follow-up ticket.
